WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.049 --> 00:00:03.340
Film Courage: When you finish a screenplay,
what do you do?

00:00:03.340 --> 00:00:09.111
Jeffrey Reddick, horror screenwriter/producer:
That’s a good question.

00:00:09.111 --> 00:00:12.250
I usually take a nap, seriously.

00:00:12.250 --> 00:00:13.519
No I do, I do.

00:00:13.519 --> 00:00:18.180
I don’t do anything crazy like celebrate.

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:22.249
I get very happy and I’m like yes and I
send it off and I usually go take a nap (especially

00:00:22.249 --> 00:00:27.149
when I’m in that last stretch where something
is either due or I’m almost done that’s

00:00:27.149 --> 00:00:30.979
where I get a little, super where I am
going back through everything and I’m paginating

00:00:30.979 --> 00:00:31.979
the pages.

00:00:31.979 --> 00:00:39.010
And one of the tricks I learned is to try
to have the last line of it on the page is

00:00:39.010 --> 00:00:43.070
something where they have literally make it
a page turner where they have to the page

00:00:43.070 --> 00:00:44.670
to see what the next page is going to be.

00:00:44.670 --> 00:00:46.510
That was a trick I learned from a friend of
mine.

00:00:46.510 --> 00:00:51.370
And so I try to go through and make sure that
unless it’s the very end of a really great

00:00:51.370 --> 00:00:54.780
scene that every end of every page is something
where you have to flip the page to at least

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:58.239
finish it because if you get them over to
the next page they’re probably going to

00:00:58.239 --> 00:00:59.239
keep reading.

00:00:59.239 --> 00:01:01.929
It’s like if you stop them at the end of
the page with something like blah they may

00:01:01.929 --> 00:01:02.949
not turn the next page.

00:01:02.949 --> 00:01:10.071
So after I’ve worked on the script itself
I then do those little mind-gamey things so

00:01:10.071 --> 00:01:12.150
to make sure it’s a page turner.

00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:16.930
By the time I’m ready to send it off I have
to go back through it one more time and then

00:01:16.930 --> 00:01:19.450
I take a nap because I’ve wore myself out.

00:01:19.450 --> 00:01:24.530
Film Courage: Also to is a sense of accomplishment
where you feel “Okay, I can rest now.”

00:01:24.530 --> 00:01:29.710
Because I’m sure up until a certain deadline
there might be a lot of sleepless nights or

00:01:29.710 --> 00:01:30.710
maybe not?

00:01:30.710 --> 00:01:34.570
Jeffrey: It’s sleepless nights and it’s
stress but when I finish something there is

00:01:34.570 --> 00:01:36.870
definitely a sense of accomplishment where
you’re like “Yes!”

00:01:36.870 --> 00:01:39.390
Especially if you know it’s good.

00:01:39.390 --> 00:01:44.410
If you’re not sure then it’s kind of like
… send (with your fingers crossed).

00:01:44.410 --> 00:01:46.840
But I’m still going to take a nap.

00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:51.750
But when you feel like you really nailed it,
it’s good but that’s again the interesting

00:01:51.750 --> 00:01:55.600
thing about the business is that you can go
a script that you’re a hundred percent confident

00:01:55.600 --> 00:02:01.240
in and your producer loved it but one person
at the studio reads it and is like “Meh?”

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:02.480
And you’re like “Ahhh…”

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:04.910
So again it’s a roller coaster ride.

00:02:04.910 --> 00:02:11.120
That’s why again I’m very big on people
doing their own content and trying to do your

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:14.380
own content because that’s if you really
want to ensure if something gets made done

00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:16.920
the way you write it, that’s the best way
to do it.

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:23.860
It’s very, very gratifying when you type
The End and you mean it and you can hit send

00:02:23.860 --> 00:02:29.510
to whoever you have to send the script over
to so you definitely feel like “Good job.”

00:02:29.510 --> 00:02:34.590
Film Courage: So if you’re not a writer-for-hire
for something and it’s your own passion

00:02:34.590 --> 00:02:37.960
project and you finish it then what’s your
process?

00:02:37.960 --> 00:02:43.650
Jeffrey: Then I usually try to send it to
my agent or manager to look at and then figure

00:02:43.650 --> 00:02:46.840
out what places to send it to.

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:52.800
Or now what I started doing because I’m
getting more into producing my own stuff is

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:58.630
connecting with some people that I think have
access to invest, more of the business side

00:02:58.630 --> 00:02:59.630
of it.

00:02:59.630 --> 00:03:02.900
Like “Okay, this might be good for this
company, these companies, so let’s try to

00:03:02.900 --> 00:03:03.959
attach somebody.”

00:03:03.959 --> 00:03:08.770
Now I’ve gotten more business-minded in
the last couple of years especially because

00:03:08.770 --> 00:03:14.650
I’d love to someday be at the point where
I’m running my own assistants and we can

00:03:14.650 --> 00:03:19.270
read a bunch of scripts too and make a whole
bunch of movies but right now it’s like

00:03:19.270 --> 00:03:24.090
I have some good stories that I want to tell
and I’m like I’ve got to maximize the

00:03:24.090 --> 00:03:27.180
chance to get those made so that means if
it’s something that I’ve written on my

00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:31.040
own, a lot of times now I’m going out and
looking for independent financing as opposed

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:32.940
to trying to get it set up somewhere.

00:03:32.940 --> 00:03:36.959
So that’s been a fun learning experience
in the last year.

